; Sub-agent sandbox profile
; =============================================================================
; Confines every coding sub-agent this project spawns to an OS sandbox.
;
; Copy this file to your config directory and edit it:
;
;     mkdir -p .config && cp example/sandbox_profile.sb.example \
;         .config/sandbox_profile.sb
;
; ``PDT_SANDBOX_PROFILE`` overrides the path.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS IS FOR
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Sub-agents are spawned as
;
;     claude --verbose --output-format stream-json \
;            --permission-mode bypassPermissions
;
; ``bypassPermissions`` removes the confirmation prompts, so without a
; profile the agent has unrestricted filesystem access and there is no gate
; anywhere in the path. A profile is the only boundary that still holds when
; the prompts are off.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; FAIL-CLOSED, AND WHY
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Configuring a profile that cannot be applied stops the run. It does not
; fall back to an unsandboxed spawn. That asymmetry is deliberate: a sandbox
; that quietly turns itself off is worse than no sandbox, because the
; configuration still claims it is on. A machine-wide wrapper once left a
; status command reporting "sandboxed" while enforcing nothing, and a plan
; then failed at its first task with
; ``sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted`` on every attempt.
;
; Not configuring a profile at all changes nothing — sub-agents run exactly
; as they always have.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; FORMAT
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Apple Seatbelt, read by ``sandbox-exec``. Rules are evaluated
; **last match wins**, so ordering is load-bearing: a broad ``deny`` placed
; after a narrow ``allow`` cancels it.
;
;   (version 1)
;   (allow default)          ; everything is permitted unless denied below
;   (deny  file-write* ...)  ; writes refused
;   (allow file-read* ...)   ; reads permitted again — must come AFTER
;                            ; the deny it is meant to override
;
; ``file-read-metadata`` grants stat/getattr without granting ``read(2)``.
; It is what lets a process resolve a path (``cd`` into a working directory)
; while the directory's contents stay unreadable.
;
; This profile is deliberately minimal: it confines writes away from the
; system and from the user's private directories, and says nothing about
; your projects. Add the rules your own layout needs.
;
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; NOTES
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; * macOS only. On any other platform a configured profile is a
;   configuration error and the run stops — ``sandbox-exec`` does not exist
;   there, and running unsandboxed because a config file was left behind is
;   the failure mode above.
; * If this process is already inside a sandbox, a nested profile is only
;   accepted when the enclosing one contains it. Pointing this at the same
;   profile the enclosing sandbox uses works; pointing it at a different
;   one stops the run with an error saying so.
; * Keep the profile free of anything specific to one machine. Paths that
;   only resolve on your checkout are configuration, not source.
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

(version 1)
(allow default)

; --- Writes: refuse the operating system's own files -----------------------
(deny file-write*
  (subpath "/System")
  (subpath "/usr")
  (subpath "/bin")
  (subpath "/sbin")
  (subpath "/Library")
  (subpath "/private/etc"))

; --- Installed applications and package managers --------------------------
; These are the "modify the machine" paths. A build that needs to write
; here is a build that has escaped its sandbox, not one to accommodate.
(deny file-write*
  (subpath "/Applications"))

; --- The user's private directories ---------------------------------------
(deny file-read* file-write*
  (subpath "/Users/you/Desktop")
  (subpath "/Users/you/Downloads")
  (subpath "/Users/you/Movies")
  (subpath "/Users/you/Music")
  (subpath "/Users/you/Pictures")
  (subpath "/Users/you/Public"))

; --- Credentials -----------------------------------------------------------
(deny file-read* file-write*
  (subpath "/Users/you/.ssh")
  (subpath "/Users/you/.gnupg")
  (subpath "/Users/you/.aws")
  (subpath "/Users/you/.kube"))

; --- Scratch space ---------------------------------------------------------
; Listed last so it wins over the system-path deny above: a sandbox that
; cannot write a temporary file cannot run a compiler.
(allow file-read* file-write*
  (subpath "/tmp")
  (subpath "/private/tmp")
  (subpath "/Users/you/.claude")
  (subpath "/Users/you/.cache")
  (subpath "/Users/you/Library/Caches"))
